Date: Thu, 07 Oct 93 00:02:38 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: Meaninglessness

I thank Dave Burns for privately forwarding his last post which I 
missed.  I think Dave and I are in general agreement, but I'll try
to round off some of the open questions:

>  How we can get a repeatable procedure for excluding weak
>  thinking that is not simply one person arbitrarily declaring
>  another's statements meaningless?

I don't think there's any single answer here.  However I'd definitely
recommend seeing how what someone's saying can be tested.  Reject
anything that smacks of circular reasoning or definitions.

>> [Newton's] premises (a belief in the bible and the wisdom of the 
>> Ancients) being flawed meant that his conclusions (Arianism, 
>> astrology and alchemy) were flawed.
> 
> Doesn't that mean that he disagreed with you (and me) on where to 
> draw the line between things that need to be operationalized and 
> things that don't?

Yes, I think so.  Certainly scholars (except for crackpot fundes) don't
validate history against the bible as Newton did (although his biblical
analyses (which supported Arianism and Unitarianism) were rather
accurate, in the sense of being validated by modern biblical scholars -
 just that they had little relevance to the real world.).

> (Cop out alert!-) If I am mistaken on Einstein and Newton, I have a
> physical chemist, Michael Polanyi, to blame...

Who is Michael Polanyi, and what does he say? - I'm curious.

>> ... someone who would say "I think strangling kittens is
>> morally wrong" or whatever.  Whereupon Perry would challenge 
>> them to define what they meant by 'right' and 'wrong'.
> 
> If the dictionary definition isn't good enough, I wonder that Perry
> doesn't use a variation of this tactic in every argument. If they
> come up with a definition, ask for a definition of subsidiary 
> concepts used in the definition, then lather, rinse, and repeat. 
> There are no primitives in natural language, so if Perry limits his
> victim to non-circular definitions there will always be an 
> undefinable object.

Clearly if we demand someone to define every word they use, recursively,
they lost in an infinite regress and the discussion becomes stymied. 
True, but I don't think Perry was doing this.  The meaning of
good/bad/evil etc is fairly central to any discussion of ethics/morals
(or whatever we're calling it today!), so it was quite a reasonable
question to ask.


>> What experiment can you perform to test whether eating fish on
>> Fridays is a sin?
> 
> (None a true extropian can perform, but a fellow Catholic could select
> a sample of test subjects and a control group, have some eat fish on
> Friday and some not, then count heads when they get to the afterlife.
> Be sure to control for acts of contrition!-)

Well I suppose it is testable, as long as the angels report back to us
:-)  But seriously, Tim Starr gave an alternative answer, which was, if
eating berries kills you, it's a sin.  This presupposes that your goal
is to stay alive, and I can think if loads of reasons why that need not
be a goal.  (We went into this extensively on the recent
AI/Slaves/Selfishness thread.)  Or if it is independent of one's goals
then this definition of sin is the equivalent death, in which case we
can drop the sin label.  So "Eating berries kills you" instead of
translating into "Eating berries is a sin" stays as "Eating berries
kills you", which strikes me as more informative.

>> two lunatics who were convinced they could speak Venusian 
>> (click bang whistle etc) and introduced them to each other (on
>> TV, I think), expecting denunciations and confusion.  Whereupon 
>> they chatted merrily away, convinced they understood each other.
> 
> Seems to me the test is not whether they think they understand each
> other, but whether both would provide similar translations of their
> conversations.

True.  Alas, I doubt that the test was done!

> Dave Burns

