Date: Thu, 7 Oct 1993 9:29:04 +0100 (BST)
From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
Subject: burglary survival (was: Extortion Counterm

Craig Presson writes:

>Just implementing a few things I learned in Basic Pistol (which my
>instructors manage to stuff quite a bit of home defense into).

>- Leaving on outside lights at night
>- Clearing away bushes and whatnot that could hide creepers near
>  windows 
>- Putting a peephole in the front door
>- Buying a gun or 3 and staying proficient, obtaining a CCW permit
>- Considering an alarm system one of these days (there are actually
>  next to no breakins in my neighborhood currently, but things will
>  get worse before they get better).

About the relative merits of the last two points ...

First a note of caution:  I live in a nation where gun control is
fairly thoroughly implemented. You can get hold of guns for target
shooting or sport (as long as they're kept in a locked cupboard at
the range) and can get a license to own and even keep one at home
(as long as it's disassembled into 2 or more pieces and kept locked
up), but the use of guns for self-defense is strongly discouraged.
(You need things like a license signed by the local chief of police,
renewed regularly, agreeing that you require a gun for self-defense
because there is a clear threat to your life. The police don't like
issuing such documents because of the implied slight to their ability
to keep the peace.) And ownership of automatic weapons is ... well,
likely to land you in prison for several years, if caught.

All this doesn't mean that there are _no_ drive-by shootings or armed
robberies in the UK -- just very few, especially by US standards. But
unarmed crimes against property are a different matter.

The house I grew up in (from age 2 to 21) was burgled 18 times.

This somewhat shocking fact (the average burglary rate in the UK
is only once per 20-25 years) can be attributed partly to the
neighbourhood -- lots of large, isolated houses, road leading
into public park, run-down areas a mile or so away -- and partly
to the layout (garden with hedge and trees between house and all
surroundings, including road and neighbours).  I'm not the spawn
of some millionaire; but when my parents bought their house they
were looking for the biggest one they could find, not the most
defensible.  They paid for their mistake over 20 years, but in
the process we learned a lot about home security -- the hard way.

Now our experience with burglaries isn't enough to create a valid
statistical sample, but it does suggest a couple of things.

1.      Burglar alarms.

        After the first (really bad) burglary we had one fitted.
        The quoted ratio of false alarms to real in the UK is
        500 to 1. My experience is that this is outrageously poor, and
        suggests that most people don't know how to use an alarm and
        that most alarms are badly installed.

        While we _did_ have false alarms, the ratio of false to real
        was about 3 to 1. In fact, on two occasions the alarm
        substantially contributed to the arrest of intruders who were
        later convicted of breaking and entering. I'd put this down
        to the way most burglar alarms are installed. Most alarms these
        days are based on IR motion sensors and are designed to
        trigger a siren as soon as anything moves. The alarm we
        had fitted relied on under-carpet pressure pads in front
        of the common burglar entry-points (vulnerable windows,
        some interior doors, top of stairs) and door-latch contacts.
        It _didn't_ ring a bell. Instead, it phoned the police
        station, and five minutes later set off an alarm. The
        theory was that a burglar who's doing a house over takes
        more than the five minutes it takes the police to arrive,
        so the alarm waits 'til the police turn up _then_ flushes
        them out. This worked, about three times. A couple of other
        times, the alarm wasn't armed properly. On at least one
        occasion it didn't go off until the burglar had left the
        premises. 

        We, of course, knew what doors not to open and where not
        to place our feet. It was possible to move around most parts
        of the house while the alarm was set -- _if_ you knew the
        layout. Burglars, however, almost invariably triggered
        the alarm -- and because there were no sensors with blinking
        lights they didn't even know one was fitted.

        The important thing to note is that a properly fitted alarm
        shouldn't generate a 500:1 ratio of false alarms -- and it
        _should_ help the police arrest a (presumably unarmed)
        burglar. This is assuming you trust the police. If you
        don't trust the police, a good alarm would page you --
        while setting off sirens left, right, and centre. That's
        probably a better bet for the present day.

2.      Windows.

        Put locks on _every_ window. I've seen the aftermath of a 
        burglar shimmying up a 12-foot plank to a 2nd story window in 
        high winds. It's not pretty: you don't want it to happen to 
        your house. Use those locks! Again, I've seen the consequence
        of leaving _one_ second-floor window unlocked (out of 17
        locks). There's no substitute for dilligence. 

3.      Shielded windows.

        If they're not visible from the road, someone may get in by
        punching out the frame. Seriously consider fitting a steel
        frame or bars if it's at ground level and invisible to the
        neighbours. (This happened three times, until we fitted
        inch-thick bars to one window that was hidden behind a tree.)

4.      Security through unstealability.

        Burglarly tends to be an opportunistic crime. It doesn't pay
        well, and the people who do it tend to be looking or
        a VCR or some loose cash or jewellery; anything they can
        dispose of easily. Either leave out _decoy_ posessions, or
        _immobilize_ valuables, or just don't leave stuff lying
        about. A house rule was never to leave money lying about;
        the result was that when one burglar was arrested, he was found
        to have successfully stolen a Spanish 500 peseta note (worth,
        literally, $5) -- which was all he had discovered. By a _decoy_
        posession I mean something that looks desirable but is really
        valueless; a dysfunctional VCR, or a big, obsolete 
        computer. For example, I leave a Mac Classic and a '386 tower 
        in my flat. My _real_ worth resides in a Powerbook which is
        worth four times as much as both those machines put 
        together ... and which is heavily concealed if/when I leave it
        unattended. If anyone takes the Classic or the '386, 
        I claim on the insurance and carry on working. They're boxes
        -- obsolete, replaceable out of petty cash.  If I had,
        say, a desktop Mac equivalent to the Powerbook, its
        theft would be quite a blow in terms of replacement costs.

        It's also quite easy to immobilize posessions. Just make them
        heavy -- a couple of lead bars in the base of a PC will
        discourage the average burglar fairly fast, for example.

5.      Neighbours.

        Do you know your neighbours? I don't. I haven't since
        I was about 15. The atomization of society is making
        neighbourhood watch type schemes increasingly unworkable,
        in my opinion. And frankly, I'd rather be self-reliant
        than have my neighbours spying on my comings and goings.

6.      Guns (NOT).

        Once, when I was 16, I was alone in the house on a hot
        summer's day. Being a nascent nerd I was sat upstairs
        in my bedroom with the door shut, plugged into headphones
        with the volume up LOUD, hacking on a Sinclair ZX-81.

        After an hour or two I came out -- to slowly discover
        signs of chaos. Open drawers. Open cupboards. Open doors.
        And downstairs, a broken window. Yes, we'd been burgled
        while I was in the house. The burglar had come in through
        a shielded downstairs window, thinking the house was
        empty (no cars parked outside) and had worked his way
        upstairs. They'd gotten as far as my parent's bedroom
        next door and been rifling the drawers when they overheard
        my headphones and bolted quietly.

        In this incident, they managed to steal nothing of any
        worth: I think they got a bead necklace belonging to my
        mother, and made a mess of some drawers. (We hadn't left
        anything theft-worthy lying around. It was instinctive, by
        then.)

        But consider this. Imagine if my father had owned a gun
        for self-defense. In the US, handguns are commonly kept
        loaded in the bedroom, in case of intrusion. Under such
        circumstances, the intruder would have found the gun 
        _before_ discovering he was not entirely alone in the house.  
        Already wired on adrenaline from fear of discovery, the
        newly armed burglar would have instantly become much more
        dangerous -- and instead of fleeing, might have been 
        sufficiently emboldened to do something stupid.

        Only stupid people burgle houses. If you keep a gun at
        home, _please_, for your own sake, make sure you don't
        leave it where someone stupid might find it in the
        process of conducting a rapid search. You wouldn't
        want to give a gun to an idiot, would you?

7.      Professionals

        We were burgled just _once_ by a team of professional
        burglars. Pray that it never happens to you.

        Professionals do not break windows. Professionals do
        not set off burglar alarms. Professionals do not
        get the neighbours interested. Professionals do not
        work alone. Typically they look like a commercial
        removals firm, come to pack your furniture up and move
        you to another city. They may keep you under observation
        and move in when you go on holiday. I know people who've
        been cleaned out that way ... while the neighbours looked
        on, not realizing that a massive burglary was in progress.
        And I mean _cleaned_ out -- they even took the carpets.

        The only two good things about professional burglars are
        that they are very rare, and they are smart enough to
        avoid dangerous situations. They won't burgle an occupied
        house and they won't risk a house that looks secure. But
        compared to the average burglar -- typically a kid with
        insufficient common sense to realize that a hot VCR is not
        worth the risk of a 5-year stretch -- professional house
        thieves are a most unpleasant experience.

8.      The present day.

        My parents sold the burglar-magnet and moved out. Since then, 
        in ten years and four households, the members of my family
        have experienced one burglary between us (or 1 per 40
        household-years). We tend to be a bit paranoid these days,
        and although we've had as many car theft problems as the
        rest of the population (about 5 stolen stereos and 2 stolen
        cars, 10-12 incidents of vandalism, between an average of
        5 vehicles over a 10 year period; this is normal in the UK)
        we take precautions in our accomodation.

        I live in a flat (apartment, in US terms) above a shop. The
        shop is on a street-corner, visible from two sides; it has
        a burglar alarm. There is a back yard and I have a back
        window overlooking it, but the window doesn't open at the
        bottom, and anyone breaking it and climbing in would 
        be visible from the street. The only ground floor entrances
        to my flat are the front door, and a door into the shop
        (covered by an IR motion sensor). Given that the shop is
        occupied during working hours -- while I am out -- anyone
        casing my flat is going to have a hard time finding a time
        when it is unoccupied and a forced entry unlikely to be
        noticed. The fourth side of the flat is a concrete wall,
        on the other side of which is a house.

        If, by some mischance, a burglar _does_ get in, they are not
        likely to find much of interest to them.  A _lot_ of paper, of
        course, and tons of books ...  but nothing obviously valuable
        except for the stereo (5 years old and not very saleable), the
        big old computers (see above), and the video/TV combo (both
        second-hand and cheap).  Any real valuables are out of sight
        and would require a serious search to detect: or they are not
        valuable in the value-system of the average burglar.  I could
        conceivably be ripped off for, say, #1000 (stereo, couple of
        computers, camera, video), but that's relatively tolerable and
        thoroughly insured.  My real worry is violence.  I value my
        life at much more than #1000.  Come to think of it, I think
        even a burglar is probably worth more than #1000 (if you can
        figure out a way to make use of them).  So if somebody breaks
        into my flat in search of property, my initial policy is to
        get them out again by any means necessary short of violence
        to either party,  then try to see they get arrested by the
        police.  But, frankly, I don't believe in turning a
        non-violent intrusion into a bloodbath by needlessly
        escallating the stakes. And I don't believe that shooting a 
        dumb kid who's only trying to steal my video recorder is a 
        fair exchange for the sleepless nights I'd have afterwards.

        I'll draw a veil over my experience some years later, while
        I was a pharmacist managing a shop which was considered a
        possible target for armed robbers. That's another matter,
        and I don't want to get into a discussion of what you do
        when a crack-crazed loony with a sawn-off shotgun comes
        through the door because the circumstances are _very_
        different from the average burglarly.

        Your mileage may vary. Me, I've been burgled the best part
        of twenty times, and this is my opinion on the basis of that
        experience. Take care, and be secure. Oh, and remember that
        this is a UK perspective. It may not be applicable in your
        country.


-- Charlie
